research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-89-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-90-## Rigorous content and proof obligations
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-91-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-92-### Principal geometry and classical equations
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-93-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-94-The first local definition must state \(P\to M\) as a smooth right principal \(G\)-bundle and distinguish the structure group \(G\), Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\), gauge group \(\mathcal G(P)\), and local gauge function. A gauge transformation of a nontrivial bundle is an equivariant bundle automorphism covering \(\mathrm{id}_M\); it is not globally an arbitrary map \(M\to G\). Such maps describe the trivialized/local expression in a chosen chart.
--
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-106-A finite-dimensional/local Faddeev–Popov identity can be proved from the inverse function theorem/change of variables when the slice intersects an orbit transversely and with the stated multiplicity. A global selection of one representative per orbit is a separate topological issue. Singer’s theorem is not an excuse to write “no gauge fixing exists” without specifying the bundle, group, topology, configuration domain and continuity. Gribov copies in a particular Coulomb/Landau condition and the global Singer obstruction are distinct statements. The Project Euclid full-paper endpoint blocked retrieval during this review; its abstract supports a narrowly qualified formulation only, and its proof is not available as a logical supplier here.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-107-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-108-For the local BRST complex, write the chosen convention at the item level. A standard infinitesimal convention is
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-109-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-110-$$sA=D_Ac,\qquad sc=-\tfrac12[c,c],\qquad s\psi=-\rho_*(c)\psi.$$
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-111-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-112-Here \(c\) is odd of ghost number \(+1\), \(A\) is even of ghost number \(0\), and \(\rho_*\) is the derivative of the specified representation. Verify the sign of \(sc\) against the selected gauge-action convention; changing \(A^u\) may change signs. Define the differential on every finite jet by commuting it with total derivatives. The nilpotence proof must compute \(s^2c\), \(s^2A\), and \(s^2\psi\), identifying exactly where Lie-algebra Jacobi and the representation identity enter. Then extend to the graded polynomial algebra. An antighost \(\bar c\) and Nakanishi–Lautrup variable \(b\) are a nonminimal contractible pair only after defining \(s\bar c=b,\; sb=0\) and proving the homotopy statement in the same jet algebra. This algebraic cohomology is distinct from physical-state cohomology \(\ker Q_{\mathrm{BRST}}/\operatorname{im}Q_{\mathrm{BRST}}\).
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-113-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:114:For BV, choose a single grading/sign convention and define fields, ghosts, antifields, antibracket, classical master action, and the allowed local functionals. In the irreducible Yang–Mills case the minimal classical master action encodes the classical action, gauge variation term \(A^*D_Ac\), and ghost-antifield term quadratic in \(c\). Prove the master equation and that its Hamiltonian vector field squares to zero by the graded Jacobi identity. State how gauge fixing is represented (for example, a canonical transformation/gauge-fixing fermion or Lagrangian submanifold) and prove only the algebraic claim actually used. Do not equate the classical master equation with a quantum measure or the quantum master equation.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-115-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-116-The existing root BV interface uses a degree-\(+1\) generator, whereas many physics references use a degree-\(-1\) BV Laplacian/bracket convention. Translate every sign, degree and \(\hbar\) power explicitly if those items are consumed. The formal QME is coefficientwise; it does not by itself construct an integral or prove convergence.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-117-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-118-### Perturbative renormalization and anomaly control
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-119-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-120-Keep three mathematical objects separate: formal Feynman coefficients after gauge fixing; locally renormalized time-ordered products/counterterms; and a nonperturbatively defined quantum theory. A perturbative gauge-renormalization item must specify the regularization scheme, gauge parameter, ghosts, matter representation, local counterterm space, ghost number, power counting, and whether the regulator preserves BRST. Prove the Ward/Slavnov–Taylor identity order by order, not by appealing to the scalar causal-extension theorem.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-121-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-122-Local anomalies are classes in the relative local complex \(H^{1,4}(s\mid d_H)\), under a declared finite-jet algebra and locality hypothesis. The Wess–Zumino consistency equation \(s a_4+d_Ha_3=0\) is necessary; exactness means a local counterterm removes the breaking. Show that the relevant obstruction class vanishes for the specified matter representation and regulator before claiming a consistent gauge theory. A semisimple non-Abelian local anomaly, mixed \(U(1)\) anomalies, and global anomalies are distinct. The global \(SU(2)\) anomaly is not detected by a local polynomial in \(H^{1,4}(s\mid d_H)\). The anomaly-matching agent owns the physical representation/defect interface; this gauge scaffold should provide the local complex, not duplicate that output.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-123-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-124-The imported root/physics scalar results can supply only the scalar/formal algebraic ingredients actually shared by the gauge calculation. **qftx-causal-renormalization** proves scalar Epstein–Glaser induction and finite renormalization composition; **qftx-renormalization-hopf** proves a combinatorial rooted-forest subtraction factorization. Neither proves non-Abelian gauge invariance of counterterms or absence of anomalies. The 1973 asymptotic-freedom papers establish a perturbative ultraviolet result under their model assumptions; they do not prove the infrared theory, confinement, a mass gap, or convergence of the perturbation series.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-125-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-126-### Topological sectors and instantons
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-127-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-128-Define Euclidean finite-action boundary data and the relation to compactification or a clutching function before defining charge. State the orientation, \(*\)-operator, structure group and invariant polynomial, curvature convention, Chern–Weil normalization, and whether the result is a real characteristic number or an integer. The current Chern–Weil theorem reaches real de Rham classes; it is not an integral-lattice theorem for arbitrary \(G\).
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-129-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:130:For a declared \(SU(2)\) or \(SU(N)\) bundle use the chosen associated bundle and its \(c_2\) convention. Prove the normalized topological charge formula and quantization from the integral characteristic class/classification assumptions; do not import \(\operatorname{tr}_{\mathrm{fund}}\) normalization without deriving the coefficient. Prove the Euclidean action inequality by decomposing \(F=F^++F^-\), identify equality with \(F=\pm *F\), then derive the classical Yang–Mills equation from Bianchi. The existence and uniqueness of a charge-one BPST representative is a separate source-backed statement: a local worked example verifies one formula but does not classify all charge-one solutions.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-131-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-132-Any semiclassical instanton contribution to a path integral additionally needs gauge zero-mode handling, the fluctuation determinant and its regulator/renormalization, collective-coordinate measure, ghost determinant, and any fermion zero modes and selection rules. If these are only a saddle expansion, label it formal/asymptotic and state the expansion parameter and domain. An instanton is a classical Euclidean solution; it is not a proof of the mass gap or confinement.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-133-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-134-### Lattice regulators, Wilson lines, and confinement
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-135-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-136-For a new non-Abelian lattice model, define a finite cell complex or graph, \(G\)-valued edge variables, vertex gauge action, ordered plaquette holonomy, Haar integration or the Hamiltonian link Hilbert space \(L^2(G)\), the exact gauge-invariant action/transfer matrix, and Gauss operators. Prove the finite-regulator gauge invariance and physical subspace separately. The imported compact \(U(1)\) rotor/CAR system is a useful analogy but is not a non-Abelian link model.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-137-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-138-Define a Wilson loop by the trace in a specified representation of the path-ordered continuum holonomy or ordered lattice edge product; prove gauge invariance by conjugation at the basepoint. A confinement criterion must say whether it is a pure-gauge center-symmetry statement, an area law for representations of nonzero \(N\)-ality, a static-source potential, or absence of colored asymptotic states. With dynamical fundamental matter, a long string may break and a Wilson area law is not a universal diagnostic. Define the infinite-loop/large-distance and continuum/infinite-volume limits, including their order.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-139-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-140-A strong-coupling area-law proof on a compact lattice is valuable and may be a theorem in its stated coupling regime. It must give an exact convergent expansion or inequalities and cannot be promoted to the asymptotically-free continuum theory without a separate continuum-limit proof. Wilson’s 1974 paper itself states that the computable strong-coupling limit loses Lorentz and Euclidean invariance. Lattice evidence, continuum-extrapolated simulations, effective models, and a mathematical construction have different evidential status.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-141-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-142-## Frontier and open-problem status
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-143-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:144:The one proposed open item is **op-qftx-clay-4d-yang-mills-existence-mass-gap**, classified as mathematics and nonlogical. The official Jaffe–Witten problem states: for every compact simple gauge group \(G\), prove existence of a nontrivial quantum Yang–Mills theory on \(\mathbb R^4\) with a positive mass gap \(\Delta>0\), with existence axioms at least as strong as Wightman or Osterwalder–Schrader. In that source, a mass gap means the Hamiltonian has no spectrum in \((0,\Delta)\) above the vacuum. The current Clay “Unsolved” archive still lists “Yang-Mills & the Mass Gap,” checked 2026-10-23. The item’s Status should preserve that date/source, cite partial finite-lattice/other-dimensional/regulator results without overgeneralizing, and state what remains required. It is not a dependency or a premise.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-145-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:146:The official Clay formulation does not ask for a proof of the Wilson-loop area law or color confinement. Jaffe–Witten discuss mass gap, confinement, and chiral symmetry breaking as separate desired quantum properties, then call confinement a natural extension of the prize problem. Keep these formulations separate. This scaffold does not create an op-item for “confinement” until the exact observable, group, matter content, limit, and current status have their own source review. A theorem awaiting local proof (classical Yang–Mills variation, BPST verification, local slice theorem, finite-lattice strong-coupling expansion) remains an authoring obligation and must not be mislabeled open.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-147-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-148-The following statuses are appropriate at this research stage:
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-149-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-150-- Classical principal-bundle identities and the charge-one BPST solution are established mathematics/physics results; the new items still owe local proofs in this library.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-151-- The Singer global-gauge-fixing obstruction is an established result with exact topological hypotheses; only its abstract was available in this review, so its full theorem statement and proof route must be checked before authoring.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-152-- Asymptotic freedom and perturbative renormalization are established within specified perturbative schemes; local non-Abelian derivations and conditions still need to be authored. Neither establishes a nonperturbative continuum limit.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:153:- Compact-lattice strong-coupling binding/area-law results are model/regime results. Do not use them as the continuum Yang–Mills mass-gap theorem.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:154:- The Clay four-dimensional existence/mass-gap problem remains officially unsolved as checked 2026-10-23.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-155-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-156-## Source ledger: exact claims and reading limits
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-157-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:158:1. **Matthijs Vákár, “Principal Bundles and Gauge Theories,” arXiv:2110.06334 (2021).** Direct targeted reading of the HTML for §1.3.1, §2.1 and §2.6.5 (not the whole thesis). §1.3.1 defines principal connection one-forms through horizontal projection and the fundamental vertical action, gives the Cartan curvature \(d\omega+\tfrac12[\omega,\omega]\), and relates principal connection equivariance to the adjoint action. §2.1 defines a gauge transformation as a principal automorphism over the identity and notes the local \(M\to G\) description in a trivialization. §2.6.5 varies the gauge-field action with compactly supported variations on relatively compact open sets and obtains the covariant Yang–Mills equation. The thesis is a mathematical exposition and includes a classical gauge-field action; it does not construct a quantum Yang–Mills theory, prove a path-integral identity, or replace this category’s local proof obligations. The imported root principal-connection items cite the corresponding printed pages 32–36 and 53–54.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-159-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:160:2. **Arthur Jaffe and Edward Witten, “Quantum Yang–Mills Theory,” official Clay problem description (2000).** Read the Clay-hosted 14-page PDF, especially §§1–2, §4 “The Problem,” and §5–6. It gives the compact-group classical curvature/action conventions, distinguishes the three quantum properties mass gap, confinement and chiral symmetry breaking, defines the mass-gap spectral condition, and states the exact compact-simple-\(G\), \(\mathbb R^4\) existence-and-gap problem. Its problem text describes mathematical construction as incomplete; the separate current Clay “Unsolved Archives” page was checked 2026-10-23. This is the authoritative problem formulation/status source, not a proof that any physical model has the stated properties.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-161-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:162:3. **Glenn Barnich, Friedemann Brandt, Marc Henneaux, “Local BRST cohomology in gauge theories,” Physics Reports 338 (2000), arXiv:hep-th/0002245.** Targeted reading of the abstract, §2.2 (Yang–Mills-type field content and BRST transformations), §2.3 (nilpotence, \(H(s)\), \(H(s\mid d)\)), §2.7 (contractible nonminimal pairs), §11 (the stated cohomology theorem for Yang–Mills-type theories without free Abelian gauge symmetries, in \(n>2\)), and §12.3 (ghost-number-one anomaly classes). The text defines a specific class of local theories: matter transforms linearly in a completely reducible representation; the Lagrangian is local and gauge invariant up to a divergence; additional matter gauge symmetries are excluded for that analysis. Its local anomaly equation is \(sa+dm=0\), with counterterms removing trivial classes. The review’s theorems have technical hypotheses and selected cases; this targeted reading is not a proof reconstruction or a blanket anomaly-cancellation theorem for any gauge/matter model. The new items must reproduce the exact chosen subcase and proof locally.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-163-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-164-4. **I. M. Singer, “Some Remarks on the Gribov Ambiguity,” Communications in Mathematical Physics 60 (1978), 7–12, DOI 10.1007/BF01609471.** The Project Euclid paper endpoint returned an anti-bot interstitial, so only its authoritative abstract/catalogue and first introductory lines were available. The abstract studies connections of a principal bundle over \(S^4\) with compact non-Abelian Lie group and states that no continuous choice of exactly one connection per orbit is possible. No details of the proof, topology on the configuration/orbit spaces, or treatment of stabilizers are imported from this read. Retrieve/read pp. 7–12 and specify those hypotheses before using it as an established theorem in active content.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-165-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:166:5. **A. A. Belavin, A. M. Polyakov, A. S. Schwartz, Yu. S. Tyupkin, “Pseudoparticle Solutions of the Yang–Mills Equations,” Physics Letters B 59 (1975), 85–87.** The full three-page primary paper was retrieved and read. In the authors’ Euclidean four-dimensional conventions, it constructs regular finite-action self-dual SU(2) charge-one pseudoparticles, identifies the charge from the asymptotic \(S^3\to SU(2)\) map, and shows that the self-dual solution saturates the action lower bound in that normalization. The paper does not provide a general moduli-space theorem and explicitly says its authors did not know whether charge \(q>1\) self-dual solutions exist at that time; do not recycle that historical uncertainty as a current open status. Translate signs and trace normalization into the library’s declared \(c_2\)/Chern–Weil convention.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-167-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-168-6. **Kenneth G. Wilson, “Confinement of Quarks,” Physical Review D 10 (1974), 2445–2459.** Read the APS abstract/metadata only, not the 15-page article. The abstract says the lattice gauge theory has a computable strong-coupling limit in which Wilson’s binding mechanism applies and free quarks are absent, while that limit lacks Lorentz and Euclidean invariance. It is cited only as a historically important regulated strong-coupling source; do not attribute a fully specified area-law proof or continuum result to the abstract.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-169-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:170:7. **David J. Gross and Frank Wilczek, “Ultraviolet Behavior of Non-Abelian Gauge Theories,” Physical Review Letters 30 (1973), 1343–1346; H. David Politzer, “Reliable Perturbative Results for Strong Interactions?,” Physical Review Letters 30 (1973), 1346–1349.** Read the APS abstracts only. Gross–Wilczek state that a wide class of non-Abelian gauge theories have free-field ultraviolet asymptotic behavior up to calculable logarithmic corrections. Politzer states a perturbative result for deep-Euclidean Green functions of Yang–Mills theories and specified fermionic extensions. The exact one-/higher-loop coefficients, scheme assumptions, and counterterms are not imported from the abstracts; derive or source them in the perturbative renormalization branch. These statements say nothing by themselves about the infrared or a convergent perturbation series.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-171-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:172:8. **G. ’t Hooft and M. Veltman, “Regularization and Renormalization of Gauge Fields,” Nuclear Physics B 44 (1972), 189–213.** Bibliographic record and abstract inspected; the full paper was not read for this scaffold. It presents dimensional regularization/renormalization methods for gauge fields and Yang–Mills-type theories. Cite it as a source to study, not as the proof of the new Slavnov–Taylor or anomaly statements.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-173-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:174:9. **V. N. Faddeev and V. N. Popov, “Feynman Diagrams for the Yang–Mills Field,” Physics Letters B 25 (1967), 29–30.** Metadata and abstract inspected; full text not read. The abstract refers to the extra loop contributions in Yang–Mills perturbation theory. The new local Faddeev–Popov identity must be derived from a stated slice/Jacobian setup; the source citation alone proves no local or global gauge-fixing theorem.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-175-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-176-## Integration and review obligations
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md-177-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:178:Before this design is made a canonical physics plan, the integrator should bind every proposed dependency to the current exact root import or to a documented planned math result with its statement, hypotheses, proof status, and consumer map. In particular: reconcile the root BV page’s item metadata/page-prose mismatch; reconcile the new root ASD items with the frozen physics import snapshot; do not treat a root draft or untracked file as a proof supplier; pin the structure group/global form and the invariant trace normalization; separate formal perturbation theory from physical assumptions and empirical evidence; retrieve Singer’s proof before stating its full theorem; and keep the Clay open problem nonlogical. A/B example pages, source status, provenance, physics scopes, item dependency roles, and axiom-base carry-through must follow physics/SCHEMA.md and physics/WORKFLOW.md. No category carrier, canonical plan, engine, or other run was changed by this note.
