+## Open problem
+
+For every compact connected Lie group $G$ whose Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$ is nonabelian and has no nonzero proper Lie ideals, construct a nontrivial four-dimensional quantum Yang–Mills theory with gauge group $G$. A Wightman realization consists of a Hilbert space $\mathcal H$, a unitary representation of the group of translations and Lorentz isometries of Minkowski space $\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with metric $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-,+,+,+)$, and local quantum fields as operator-valued tempered distributions acting on a common dense invariant domain; the fields transform covariantly, and gauge-invariant fields localized at regions with spacelike separation $\eta(x-y,x-y)>0$ commute. Their correlation functions are the vacuum expectation distributions of finite products of smeared fields. The self-adjoint Hamiltonian $H$ generating time translations has spectrum $\sigma(H)\subseteq[0,\infty)$, and the theory has a unique Poincaré-invariant vacuum vector $\Omega$ up to phase with $H\Omega=0$. Alternatively, a Euclidean construction gives Schwinger distributions, meaning Euclidean vacuum correlation distributions on $\mathbb R^4$, satisfying the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms (temperedness, Euclidean covariance, reflection positivity, permutation symmetry, and clustering) strongly enough for reconstruction of a Wightman theory. The required nontrivial gauge content includes local operator fields corresponding to gauge-invariant local polynomials in a smooth connection's curvature and its covariant derivatives, with short-distance correlations prescribed by asymptotic freedom (the perturbative prediction that the gauge coupling tends to zero at short distances) and perturbative renormalization, including a stress tensor and an operator product expansion. Here the curvature of a smooth local connection $A$ is $F_A=dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]$, its covariant derivative on adjoint-valued forms is $D_A=d+[A,\cdot]$, and an operator product expansion is a short-distance expansion of products of local fields in local fields. The required mass gap is a finite number $\Delta>0$ such that $\sigma(H)\cap(0,\Delta)=\varnothing$, using units $c=\hbar=1$. Confinement is a separate question and is not part of this problem.
+
+## Open problem references
+
+[Clay Mathematics Institute, “Yang-Mills & the Mass Gap” (current problem and status page; checked 2026-10-23)](https://www.claymath.org/millennium/yang-mills-the-maths-gap/)
+
+[Arthur Jaffe and Edward Witten, “Quantum Yang-Mills Theory,” in *The Millennium Prize Problems*, §§3–6, printed pp. 134–150](https://www.claymath.org/library/monographs/MPPc.pdf)
+
+[Arthur Jaffe, “Constructive Quantum Field Theory,” §7.3, “For the Millennium: Gauge Theory in Four Dimensions,” PDF pp. 13–15](https://arthurjaffe.net/Assets/pdf/CQFT.pdf)
+
+## Status
+
+The Clay Mathematics Institute page was checked on 2026-10-23 and labels the problem unsolved, stating that no proof of the mass-gap property is known. Jaffe and Witten distinguish this existence-and-gap task from confinement and chiral symmetry breaking, which they describe as separate extensions. Their mathematical perspective records established constructive results mainly in lower dimensions or compact approximations; it says that the four-dimensional Yang–Mills construction on a compact four-torus is incomplete and that a uniform gap and the infinite-volume limit to $\mathbb R^4$ remain required. Jaffe's earlier lecture notes give an $SU(2)$ formulation and describe partial progress while leaving the infrared limit unresolved. These statements do not promote finite-regulator models, formal perturbation series, simulations, or lower-dimensional constructions to a continuum solution on $\mathbb R^4$.
