+
+## Example
+
+Let $\mathbb H$ be the real quaternion algebra with basis $1,i,j,k$, multiplication $i^2=j^2=k^2=ijk=-1$, and norm $|a+bi+cj+dk|^2=a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2$. Let $SU(2)$ be its unit quaternions and let $SO(3)$ be the group of orientation-preserving linear isometries of $\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{i,j,k\}\cong\mathbb R^3$. A real Lie algebra is the tangent space of a smooth Lie group at its identity with the bracket induced by left-invariant vector fields; it is simple if it is nonabelian and has no nonzero proper ideals. For this example, a compact simple gauge group means a compact connected Lie group with a simple Lie algebra. Its global form means the Lie group itself, including its central quotient, rather than only its Lie algebra. The map $\pi:SU(2)\to SO(3)$ defined by $\pi(q)x=qxq^{-1}$ is a surjective homomorphism with kernel $\{1,-1\}$; its differential identifies the simple Lie algebras of $SU(2)$ and $SO(3)$, while the groups are distinct. Therefore, a proof of a claim quantified over every compact simple gauge group at $SU(2)$ alone does not discharge the separate $SO(3)$ case.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Arthur Jaffe and Edward Witten, “Quantum Yang-Mills Theory,” §4, “The Problem”](https://www.claymath.org/library/monographs/MPPc.pdf)
+
+[Arthur Jaffe, “Constructive Quantum Field Theory,” §7.3, “For the Millennium: Gauge Theory in Four Dimensions”](https://arthurjaffe.net/Assets/pdf/CQFT.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Use the quaternion algebra and Lie-group conventions stated in the Example.
+
+[A1] The norm on $\mathbb H$ is multiplicative, and the imaginary quaternions have the Euclidean inner product for which $i,j,k$ are an orthonormal basis.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Conjugation by a unit quaternion preserves $\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H$ and its norm because $|q x q^{-1}|=|q||x||q^{-1}|=|x|$. The map $q\mapsto\pi(q)$ is a homomorphism by associativity. Its determinant is $+1$: it depends continuously on $q$, takes values in $\{-1,+1\}$, and equals $+1$ at $q=1$; the unit quaternions form the connected sphere $S^3$. For any $R\in SO(3)$, its eigenvalues have modulus one and nonreal eigenvalues occur in conjugate pairs. Since $\det R=1$, it has a unit fixed axis $n$; on $n^\perp$ it is a planar rotation through some angle $\theta$. The quaternion $q=\cos(\theta/2)+n\sin(\theta/2)$ acts by conjugation as that rotation, as follows by expanding $q x q^{-1}$ with the quaternion multiplication rules. Thus $\pi$ is onto. [given, A1, algebra]
+
+1.2 If $\pi(q)$ is the identity, then $q$ commutes with every imaginary quaternion. Commuting with both $i$ and $j$ forces $q$ to be real, so the unit condition gives $q=\pm1$; hence $\ker\pi=\{1,-1\}$. The differential at the identity sends $v\in\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H$ to the linear map $x\mapsto[v,x]=2v\times x$. It is injective, and both Lie algebras have dimension three, so it is an isomorphism. The cross-product bracket is simple: if an ideal contains $v\ne0$, then it contains $w\times v$ for every $w$, which spans $v^\perp$; together with $v$ this is all of $\mathbb R^3$. Therefore both Lie algebras are nonabelian and simple. [given, A1, algebra]
+
+1.3 The center of $SU(2)$ is $\{\pm1\}$, since an element commuting with every imaginary quaternion must be real. If $R$ is in the center of $SO(3)$, it commutes with every half-turn about an axis $n$; each such half-turn has the line $\mathbb R n$ as its $+1$ eigenspace, so $R$ preserves every line. A linear isometry preserving every line is scalar, and the only scalar in $SO(3)$ is the identity. The centers therefore differ, proving the groups are not isomorphic. Since $\pi$ is a surjective local isomorphism with kernel $\{\pm1\}$, they are distinct compact global forms with isomorphic simple Lie algebras. [given, A1, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+2.1 The universal group quantifier includes both of these distinct groups. A proof with the fixed value $G=SU(2)$ establishes only that instance; it contains no argument for the separate value $G=SO(3)$. Hence it does not by itself establish the universally quantified claim. [given, step 1.3, algebra] ∎
+
+## Remarks
+
+This example clarifies the universal group scope of [[op-qftx-clay-4d-yang-mills-existence-mass-gap]]; the open problem is only a contextual reference and supplies no premise to this proof.
+
+## Remarks references
+
