+
+## Example
+
+Let $\Gamma$ be the finite oriented boundary cycle of a square, with four edges $e_1,e_2,e_3,e_4$ and one plaquette. Use the compact matrix group $G=SU(2)=\left\{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\-\overline b&\overline a\end{pmatrix}:a,b\in\mathbb C,\ |a|^2+|b|^2=1\right\}$ in its defining two-dimensional representation. Assign one link variable $U_j\in G$ to each edge and set the ordered plaquette holonomy to $U_p=U_1U_2U_3^{-1}U_4^{-1}$. The group is the unit sphere $S^3$ in the $(a,b)$ coordinates; let $\mu$ be its normalized round surface measure. For $\beta\ge0$, define the dimensionless action $S_\beta(U)=\beta\left(1-\tfrac12\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{Tr}U_p\right)$ and partition function $Z_\beta=\int_{G^4}e^{-S_\beta(U)}\,d\mu^{\otimes4}(U)$. This finite graph integral satisfies $e^{-2\beta}\le Z_\beta\le1$, so it is finite and strictly positive. It has only four group variables and defines no continuum fields, no Wightman or Osterwalder–Schrader reconstruction, and no Hamiltonian; it therefore does not solve the four-dimensional existence and mass-gap problem.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Arthur Jaffe and Edward Witten, “Quantum Yang-Mills Theory,” §§4–6, printed pp. 141–150](https://www.claymath.org/library/monographs/MPPc.pdf)
+
+[Arthur Jaffe, “Constructive Quantum Field Theory,” §7.3, “For the Millennium: Gauge Theory in Four Dimensions”](https://arthurjaffe.net/Assets/pdf/CQFT.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Use the finite graph, group, edge orientation, and $\beta\ge0$ stated in the Example.
+
+[A1] Each matrix in $G$ has the displayed form with $|a|^2+|b|^2=1$, so $\operatorname{Tr}U=2\operatorname{Re}a$.
+
+[A2] The measure $\mu$ is normalized round surface volume on the unit sphere in $\mathbb C^2$, and $\mu^{\otimes4}$ is the corresponding fourfold product probability measure.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Products and inverses of matrices in $G$ remain in $G$, so $U_p\in G$. By [A1], $\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{Tr}U_p=2\operatorname{Re}a_p$ for some $a_p$ with $|a_p|\le1$. Hence $-2\le\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{Tr}U_p\le2$, and the defined action satisfies $0\le S_\beta(U)\le2\beta$. [given, A1, algebra]
+
+1.2 Left and right multiplication by any fixed $V\in G$ preserve the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product on complex $2\times2$ matrices, because $V$ is unitary. They restrict to isometries of the embedded sphere $G\cong S^3$ and preserve its normalized round volume. Thus $\mu$ is a left- and right-invariant probability measure, and the product measure in [A2] has total mass one. [given, A2, algebra]
+
+2.1 Exponentiating the action bounds gives $e^{-2\beta}\le e^{-S_\beta(U)}\le1$ for every $U\in G^4$. The integrand is continuous, hence measurable and integrable on the finite probability space $(G^4,\mu^{\otimes4})$. Integrating the pointwise bounds yields $e^{-2\beta}\le Z_\beta\le1$, which proves finiteness and strict positivity. At $\beta=0$, the integrand is identically one and $Z_0=1$. [given, A1, A2, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+2.2 The defined object is one integral over the finite-dimensional space $G^4$; its definition contains no fields on $\mathbb R^4$, no limiting construction, no reconstructed Hilbert space, and no Hamiltonian whose spectrum could satisfy the required gap condition. It establishes only the finite-regulator integral asserted in the Example, not a continuum Yang–Mills theory or a mass gap. [given, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
+
+## Remarks
+
+This finite construction is a regulated example related to [[op-qftx-clay-4d-yang-mills-existence-mass-gap]], not a logical approximation theorem or a supplier of the open conclusion.
+
+## Remarks references
