items/ex-qftx-clay-two-global-forms-same-algebra.md:30:Let $\mathbb H$ be the real quaternion algebra with basis $1,i,j,k$, multiplication $i^2=j^2=k^2=ijk=-1$, and norm $|a+bi+cj+dk|^2=a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2$. A Lie group is a finite-dimensional smooth manifold with smooth multiplication and inversion; its Lie algebra is its tangent space at the identity with the bracket induced by left-invariant vector fields. Let $SU(2)$ be the group of unit quaternions and $SO(3)$ the group of linear maps of $\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{i,j,k\}\cong\mathbb R^3$ preserving the Euclidean inner product and having determinant $+1$. A Lie algebra is simple if it is nonabelian and has no nonzero proper ideals. For this example, a compact simple gauge group means a compact connected Lie group with a simple Lie algebra. Its global form means the Lie group itself, including its central quotient, rather than only its Lie algebra. The kernel of a group homomorphism is the inverse image of the identity, and the center is the set of elements commuting with every group element. The map $\pi:SU(2)\to SO(3)$ defined by $\pi(q)x=qxq^{-1}$ is a surjective homomorphism with kernel $\{1,-1\}$; its differential identifies the simple Lie algebras of $SU(2)$ and $SO(3)$, while the groups are distinct. Therefore, a proof of a claim quantified over every compact simple gauge group at $SU(2)$ alone does not discharge the separate $SO(3)$ case.
items/ex-qftx-clay-two-global-forms-same-algebra.md:42:[A1] The norm on $\mathbb H$ is multiplicative, and the imaginary quaternions have the Euclidean inner product for which $i,j,k$ are an orthonormal basis.
items/ex-qftx-clay-two-global-forms-same-algebra.md:46:1.1 Conjugation by a unit quaternion preserves $\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H$ and its norm because $|q x q^{-1}|=|q||x||q^{-1}|=|x|$. The map $q\mapsto\pi(q)$ is a homomorphism by associativity. Its determinant is $+1$: it depends continuously on $q$, takes values in $\{-1,+1\}$, and equals $+1$ at $q=1$; the unit quaternions form the connected sphere $S^3$. For any $R\in SO(3)$, its eigenvalues have modulus one and nonreal eigenvalues occur in conjugate pairs. If there is a nonreal pair, the remaining real eigenvalue is $+1$ because the determinant is $+1$; if all eigenvalues are real, they are $\pm1$ and their product $+1$ forces at least one eigenvalue $+1$. Thus $R$ has a unit fixed axis $n$. Its restriction to $n^\perp$ preserves orientation, since its determinant there equals $\det R=1$, and so it is a planar rotation through some angle $\theta$. Expanding with $n x=-n\cdot x+n\times x$ gives $q x q^{-1}=\cos(\theta)x+\sin(\theta)(n\times x)+(1-\cos(\theta))(n\cdot x)n$ for $q=\cos(\theta/2)+n\sin(\theta/2)$, which is precisely that rotation. Thus $\pi$ is onto. [given, A1, algebra]
items/ex-qftx-clay-two-global-forms-same-algebra.md:48:1.2 If $\pi(q)$ is the identity, then $q$ commutes with every imaginary quaternion. Commuting with both $i$ and $j$ forces $q$ to be real, so the unit condition gives $q=\pm1$; hence $\ker\pi=\{1,-1\}$. The differential at the identity sends $v\in\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H$ to the linear map $x\mapsto[v,x]=2v\times x$. It is injective because $v\times x=0$ for every $x$ forces $v=0$. The tangent space of $SU(2)\cong S^3$ has dimension three, and the tangent space of $SO(3)$ at the identity consists of the skew-symmetric $3\times3$ matrices, also dimension three; therefore the differential is an isomorphism. It preserves brackets because it is the differential of a Lie-group homomorphism. The cross-product bracket is simple: if an ideal contains $v\ne0$, then it contains $w\times v$ for every $w$, which spans $v^\perp$; together with $v$ this is all of $\mathbb R^3$. Therefore both Lie algebras are nonabelian and simple. [given, A1, algebra]

